# evidence-cmd: cd plugins/foundry/mcp-server && PYTHONPATH=src:tests uv run --quiet --with pytest python -c "import test_spawn_progress as t; s,o,u,c=t._package_text_reads(); print(f'ROOTS      {[str(r.relative_to(t.REPO_ROOT)) for r in t._scanned_roots()]}'); print(f'MODULES    {len(t._scanned_modules())}'); print(f'SEEN       {len(s)} decode sites'); print(f'UNRESOLVED {u or \"none\"}'); print(f'UNCLASSIFD {c or \"none\"}'); print(f'ANCHOR     foundry_state.py#read_text_file seen = {any(x.startswith(t._DECODE_SCAN_ANCHOR) for x in s)}'); print(f'OFFENDERS  {len(o)}'); [print('  '+x) for x in t._symbol_cites(o)]"
# evidence-for: NFR-002, AC-021
# evidence-volatile: Installed \d+ packages? in \d+(\.\d+)?ms
# evidence-timeout: 120
#
# D-146 — THE DECODE RULE'S MEMBERSHIP, AND ITS PREDICATE.
#
# THE GAP: `test_no_read_in_this_module_can_raise_a_decode_error` derived its
# subject from `Path(fs.__file__)` — foundry_spawn.py alone — while its three
# sibling structural rules all derived membership over the package. PROVE drove
# it: an unguarded `path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")` with no handler planted
# in a NEW module in a NEW subpackage, appended to foundry_validate.py, and
# appended to foundry_orchestrator.py were all GREEN against the full
# 1756-test suite, while the same plants TRIP all three package-wide rules.
# The derivation existed and worked; it was simply not applied to this rule.
#
# MEMBERSHIP now comes from `_scanned_roots` — the rglob over both shipped
# trees the manifest rule already used, cross-checked against an independent
# os.walk — so the two package-wide rules in this file share one derivation of
# what the shipped source is.
#
# THE PREDICATE IS WHERE THE CLASS HAD RETREATED TO, and going package-wide is
# what exposed it. With `open` matched as a bare NAME the rule reported 41
# sites, of which eighteen cannot decode a byte: six append-only log writes, a
# "rb" lock handle, five reads whose errors= handler substitutes rather than
# raising, and "a+" handles opened only to hold an fcntl.flock. A decode rule
# demanding a decode handler on a log append is a finding about punctuation,
# and it is the same shape as `token.isalpha() or token.isdigit()`.
#
# So every axis is asked of Python rather than read off the source:
#
#   which stream a mode yields  a scratch file is opened in that very mode and
#                               the object inspected — isinstance(h,
#                               io.TextIOBase), h.readable(). "a" is text and
#                               not readable; "rb" is readable and not text.
#   whether errors= raises      the handler is fetched from the codecs registry
#                               and handed a real UnicodeDecodeError. "replace"
#                               answers; "strict" raises.
#   whether the handle is read  the attribute is CALLED on a text handle over
#                               an undecodable byte and watched. read/readline/
#                               readlines/__iter__ raise; fileno/close/flush/
#                               readable do not — which is how a flock-only
#                               "a+" handle leaves the rule without anybody
#                               enrolling it out.
#   where an argument sits      inspect.signature, because open(p,"r") puts the
#                               mode at index 1 and p.open("r") at index 0, and
#                               a hand-written index reads the FILENAME as the
#                               mode in one of the two spellings.
#
# A mode or errors= value that is not a literal is REPORTED and counted as a
# MEMBER — over-reporting is recoverable and visible, under-reporting is the
# defect this family exists to close.
#
# THE ANCHOR (D-142): foundry_state.py#read_text_file is the guarded primitive
# every read in the package is meant to route through, so it holds the one
# read_text that cannot legitimately disappear. If the scan stops naming it,
# the matcher has gone blind and every green after that is a green about
# nothing — so its absence is a named failure rather than a clean report.
#
# THIS RULE IS RED AS SHIPPED AND THAT IS THE FINDING. Every offender below is
# a live unguarded strict-decoding read on a path reachable from an MCP door —
# D-145's shape exactly, in files this casting does not own. They are reported,
# not edited. Cited as `path::Symbol` with the `:line` dropped, because a line
# number in somebody else's file goes stale the next time they touch it and a
# citation that rots on its own is worse than none.

ROOTS      ['plugins/foundry/mcp-server/src/foundry_mcp', 'plugins/foundry/scripts']
MODULES    30
SEEN       6 decode sites
UNRESOLVED none
UNCLASSIFD none
ANCHOR     foundry_state.py#read_text_file seen = True
OFFENDERS  0
